#: ../../examples/notebooks/10_bench_operation.ipynb:50002
msgid "The equipment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/10_bench_operation.ipynb:50004
msgid "In a laboratory, the devices are plugged into the computer. Here we start their emulators in this process (`lumos emulators start` does the same from a terminal) and **discover** them: LUMOS asks every address `*IDN?` and keeps the devices that answer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/10_bench_operation.ipynb:70002
msgid "UC3: one bench, simulated then operated"
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/10_bench_operation.ipynb:70004
msgid "A laser goes through a shutter and a lens carried by a stage, which focuses it on a camera. `ctx.link` makes the propagation distance follow the stage, so that the twin sees the same focus as the bench. The camera is described with the nominal parameters of the `LL-CAM` (gain, bias, dark current, read noise)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/10_bench_operation.ipynb:90002
msgid "The same sequence is replayed on the equipment. Switching the laser on is a dangerous action: the driver asks for a confirmation (here a function that accepts; in the web interface or through an AI assistant, a human answers). `operation_guard` brings everything to a safe state if anything goes wrong."
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/10_bench_operation.ipynb:120002
msgid "Safety"
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/10_bench_operation.ipynb:120004
msgid "Every command is checked against the limits of its driver, read from the device, even when it is sent directly to the driver. An emergency stop interrupts a motion in progress, from any thread."
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/10_bench_operation.ipynb:140002
msgid "UC4: calibrating the phase of a chip"
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/10_bench_operation.ipynb:140004
msgid "A laser injected in a fibre is split into the two arms of a Bracewell combiner. One arm has a fabrication error of 0.4 rad; a heater driven by the `LL-PCU` controller corrects it (phase κV²). The dark output is read by the `LL-PM` power meter. The calibration scans the heater phase and keeps the deepest null, **measured on the equipment**; the twin shows what the model predicts at the same settings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../examples/notebooks/10_bench_operation.ipynb:170002
msgid "The measured null follows the twin, up to the gain error (2 %), the offset (1 nW) and the noise of the power meter: the controlled gap between the bench and its model."
msgstr ""

